# Every ameba SoC loses its system timer across a sleep -- the Cortex-M ones stop
# SysTick (clock-gating takes the core clock away, power-gating takes the core),
# and on the CA32 the generic timer's counter comes back from reset with the
# cluster. So the driver has to restart it and reconstruct the elapsed time on
# wake, which is what these two say.
#
# Hooks rather than the Counter API variant on all of them, because the reference
# is the always-on ameba system timer (TIM0) and it is only ever read: a companion
# device would additionally be armed and reprogrammed each idle, and TIM0 cannot
# serve as both the reference and that. It is also, on some of these SoCs, the only
# basic timer wired to the wake logic, so handing it to the counter driver would
# stop the very thing being read.
#
# The hooks themselves live with each SoC's sleep entry: common/pm.c for the
# Cortex-M SoCs, amebasmart/pm.c for the CA32.
#
# Guarded on PM rather than per SoC: no ameba SoC keeps its system timer across a
# sleep, and a target built without CONFIG_PM never sleeps in the first place.

choice SYSTEM_TIMER_LPM_COMPANION
	default SYSTEM_TIMER_LPM_COMPANION_HOOKS if PM
endchoice

config SYSTEM_TIMER_RESET_BY_LPM
	default y if PM
